\f0\fs32 \cf0 Our project uses the life expectancy files, which contain tract data across WA state from 2010-2015, measuring life expectancy on birth.\
\
There are seven database fields, four of which are character about the specific unique IDs for each state, county and census (TRACT ID), which are are listed as unique databases. Then, there are three numeric database types, one for the life expectancy at birth, another for that life expectancy at birth\'92s standard error, and another for indicating the source of those age-specific death rates used to calculated abridged period life tables.\
\
\
The CDC estimates life expectancy across all age groups by using three estimate flags, one uses observed death rates, the second uses predicted death rates, and the third combines both observed and predicted death rates. These are matched to their corresponding estimate flags of 1, 2, and 3. The CDC also uses standard error to measure the variance for the data reporting probability of age-specific death rates. \
\
In this context, out of 1370 tracts, there were 1169 \'933\'94 flags, 112 \'932\'94 flags, and 79 \'931\'94 flags, while the standard error ranges from 0.76 to 3.93 with an average of 1.84. In other words, most of the data is relatively consistent withs some skew, and mostly consists of both observations and predictions (reliant on statistical modeling). These differences could be due to the varying population sizes and demographics within each tract.\
\
\
\
Discrepancy of 88 tract data with missing point estimates:\
\
88 tract data data may have been excluded due to a lack of criteria requirements being fulfilled, such as large enough population sizes, low standard errors, and/or complete geocoding of the census data. \
